From fd7c4e1bfca47ec4d798b9c064af1960c00b4fdb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: axfor <aixiaoxiang2009@hotmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 09:25:18 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 27/40] lib/streamaggr: write sums at the timestamps of the
 samples, not of the flush

Stream aggregation stamps every output with the time of the flush that
writes it. When vmagent falls behind, a round scraped at 10:59 is
aggregated and written at 11:02, and its usage counts towards the wrong
minute, or the wrong hour once a recording rule cuts the series by hour.

The new sample_timestamps option, also set for all aggregations it applies
to by -remoteWrite.streamAggr.sampleTimestamps and -streamAggr.sampleTimestamps,
writes sum_samples and sum_samples_total at the timestamps of their input
samples instead:

- sum_samples writes one sum per distinct sample timestamp. A sum arriving
  after its interval was already written goes out as one more sample a few
  milliseconds earlier, so that deduplication keeps both and
  sum_over_time() adds them up.
- sum_samples_total writes the total as of each scrape round, in timestamp
  order. Written timestamps never go back, which would look like a counter
  reset, so a round that arrives after a later one was written goes to that
  later one: the error is bounded by how far the targets are apart, not by
  how far the aggregator is behind. The heartbeat goes out at the latest
  sample timestamp seen rather than at the wall clock, and the reset marker
  before a new output precedes its first sample.

The per-aggregation option refuses outputs, enable_windows and
dedup_interval it cannot honour; the flags skip such aggregations. Without
the option nothing changes, and the new state lives in separate types, so
the default path keeps its memory per output series.

The scrape loop also stamped a round with its tick even when the tick had
waited behind a slow previous round, counting the time in between towards
the past. A tick more than a tenth of the interval late is now replaced by
the time the scrape actually starts.
